            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest Mars Perseverance rover news is that NASA reports Perseverance has been studying some of the oldest terrain ever examined by a Mars rover: a layered rock sequence on the rim of Jezero Crater called the “Broom Point member,” likely more than 3.9 billion years old. The findings suggest a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of ancient rock was built up by repeated asteroid impacts, preserving a record from a very early, violent period in solar-system history【6:2†source】.\n\nNASA says Perseverance began examining this region after climbing the western rim of Jezero Crater in late 2024. Instruments found multiple rock types, including breccias and fine-grained pulverized layers, plus glassy beads that point more strongly to impact processes than volcanism【6:2†source】. The significance is that Mars’ lack of plate tectonics may have preserved ancient geology that Earth has largely erased【6:2†source】.\n\nAnother recent milestone: Perseverance has now driven the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — reaching that mark on sol 1,890 of the mission, about five years and four months after landing. That was much faster than Opportunity, which took over 11 years to cover the same distance【6:0†source】. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter also captured Perseverance and its tracks from orbit near the “Arbot” region west of Jezero Crater shortly before the milestone【6:0†source】.\n\nNASA’s Perseverance news page also lists fresh July 15 updates showing a sweeping view and route information for Broom Point, along with the impact-record discovery【6:3†source】.",
                "annotations": [
